batch_6_sections = {
    # PART XIX: REGULATIONS, STANDARDS AND DELEGATED LEGISLATION
    "Power to make regulations": "(1) The Cabinet Secretary may, on the recommendation of the Council, make regulations generally for the better carrying out of the provisions of this Act.\n(2) Without prejudice to the generality of subsection (1), regulations may prescribe the procedure for the Tribunal, the specific limits of decennial liability, and the parameters of the safety levy.",
    "Standards": "The Council shall develop, publish, and continuously update binding technical standards and codes of practice governing the minimum acceptable quality of work for all built environment professions.",
    "Consultation": "Before advising the Cabinet Secretary to make any regulations under this Act, the Council must formally consult with the relevant Profession-Specific Boards and the Council of Governors.",
    "Public participation": "Any proposed regulations, statutory scales of fees, or major policy shifts by the Council must be subjected to a minimum of thirty days of robust public participation in accordance with the Constitution.",
    "Publication": "All regulations, standards, and codes of practice made under this Act must be published in the Gazette and made freely accessible on the Council's digital portal.",
    "Review": "The Council shall undertake a mandatory, comprehensive review of all regulations and statutory scales of fees at least once every five years to ensure they remain economically viable and technically relevant.",
    "Limits on delegated powers": "For the avoidance of doubt, no regulation made under this Act shall create a criminal offence punishable by a term of imprisonment exceeding six months, nor shall any regulation levy a tax not explicitly authorized by primary legislation.",

    # PART XX: TRANSITIONAL AND CONSEQUENTIAL PROVISIONS
    "Existing registrations": "Any person who, immediately before the commencement of this Act, was registered under any of the repealed Acts shall automatically be deemed to be registered under this Act in the corresponding category.",
    "Existing practising certificates": "A practising certificate issued under any of the repealed Acts and valid immediately before the commencement of this Act shall remain valid until its original expiry date, after which renewal shall be governed by this Act.",
    "Existing professionals": "All professionals recognized by existing statutory boards shall transition into the new unified National Register without the need for re-examination, provided their historical CPD records are compliant.",
    "Existing technicians and technologists": "Technicians and technologists currently registered with the Board of Registration of Architects and Quantity Surveyors or the Engineers Board of Kenya shall be migrated to their respective standalone categories in the new Register.",
    "Existing firms": "All professional firms and partnerships registered prior to this Act shall be deemed to be registered under this Act and must update their digital records within one year of commencement.",
    "Existing examinations": "Any candidate currently undertaking professional examinations under the repealed Acts shall be permitted to complete their examination cycle under the old rules for a transitional period of two years.",
    "Existing disciplinary matters": "Any disciplinary proceedings initiated under the repealed Acts and pending before the commencement of this Act shall be concluded by the new Council's Disciplinary Committee as if they had been initiated under this Act.",
    "Existing regulations": "All regulations, rules, and guidelines made under the repealed Acts shall, to the extent that they are not inconsistent with this Act, remain in force until they are revoked or replaced by new regulations under this Act.",
    "Transitional licences": "The Council may issue transitional, restricted licences to persons who have been practicing informally but do not meet the strict new academic criteria, giving them a three-year grace period to upgrade their qualifications.",
    "Repeal of Cap. 525": "The Architects and Quantity Surveyors Act (Cap. 525) is hereby repealed.",
    "Consequential amendments": "The written laws specified in the Seventh Schedule are amended in the manner specified in that Schedule to align them with the establishment of the unified Council.",
    "Savings provisions": "The repeal of the Acts specified in the Eighth Schedule shall not affect any acquired rights, privileges, obligations, or liabilities accrued under those Acts prior to their repeal.",
    "Review of Act": "The Cabinet Secretary shall ensure that a comprehensive review of the operation and impact of this Act is conducted within five years of its commencement, and a report submitted to Parliament."
}
